Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-12T08:26:02-08:00","id":403344,"title":"","content":"One of my oldest friends, whom I got in contact again because of Facebook, came out to me telling me she is a Transgender just less than 2 months ago. She was born a male but since she is transgender sees herself as woman, I am honoring what she identifies with just so I am not going to confuse anyone on this. <br /> <br /> I have had friends who are gay, they have usually never hidden who they are and one came out. I have always supported and been there for them. I have know the coming out stories and even had one friend stay on my couch when her family kicked her out until she was able to get on her feet. <br /> <br /> I have never known a Transgender before. When she told me who she discovered who she was and all the pain, she never told me about even when we knew each other 20 years ago. That she could not hide who she was. It was tough on her. I just asked is she happy. She said yes for the first time in her life. I said good that is all that matters to me. You are still my friend and when we get together I am more than happy to introduce you to my family and child. <br /> <br /> She cried. She said that when she came out a few years ago, she expected her family especially her twin (who was born a woman and identifies herself as one) to not accept her. She knew the stories and how bad it would be. Her family excepted her and helped her be her. She could not believe it. So hard it is for Transgenders to do this and to get family support is extremely rare. She has lost some people in her life whom she thought were her friends and those that truly showed whom they are. <br /> <br /> She telling me who she was, she was not sure I would accept her. When I did she cried. I even introduced her on the phone with my wife. Isabella which is her name now is still my friend. She is happy to answer my questions and is happy because no one has been that interested to ask all these things. She honestly looks better as a woman. To me it seems right and perfect for my friend. She is still the same person I knew before. Same jokes and everything, but more confident than I have ever seen her. <br /> <br /> I will say knowing Isabella as she talks about her community I never knew about and the things she has gone through and other Transgenders she knows, I am learning a lot. I am still learning so I hope I am not offending anyone with what I said. I asked Isabella if I could say this and her first name. She said it was fine.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4949-nene-richards","name":"Nene Richards","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4949_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"70"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-18T16:38:56-08:00","id":410732,"title":"","content":"I first realized I was bisexual when I was 13 years old, 8th grade, and lost my virginity at the same time while experimenting. At first I did it with a girl then a guy. To my surprise I liked it very much. And that's when I realized what I am. I even found out I like bara, masculine natural bulky men. I have never told anyone that I'm bisexual because I was really afraid and found no good reason to tell. Until recently, last year I finally told my friends who I confide very much. I was a nervous wreck at first until my friends said, &quot;so, good for you. Nothing to be ashamed of.&quot; That's when I felt complete, a place where I longed for. I even felt flattered when they asked many questions about my personal sex life with men. To be honest I have never been in a relationship with a man, I found no reason for it when I was discrete. I just had many one-night-stands and booty calls with guys. For girls it was the same except I was in a couple of relationships. As for the part to tell my parents about it? No, no reason for it at all. I come from a Mexican family where my father is a total conservative and will never accept the new age. My mother would probably accept it but would be a little sad. And to add it I'm the only male to continue the family's last name, so yeah, no point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2360-cecoyotl","name":"Cecoyotl","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2360_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-19T12:51:22-08:00","id":411932,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cecoyotl</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/58142-the-lgbt-thread?p=410732#post410732\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I even found out I like bara, masculine natural bulky men.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm also bara loving bear bi guy! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhams